(defmodule perf
  "One-call performance triage: `(perf/report)` answers *where the time goes*.

The counters and the GC snapshot were always available — `(vm-stats)` and `(gc-stats)`
— but reading them meant knowing which of ~75 `BROOD_*` flags to set, which cargo
feature to build, and the interpretation rules in `docs/benchmarking.md` §2. This
module carries the interpretation so it does not have to be recalled.

- `(perf/report)` — a map: the two snapshots, the derived ratios, and a `:verdict`.
- `(perf/summary)` — the same, printed for a human.

**This is a counting tool, not a timing one.** The counters are atomics on the hot
path, so they perturb timing; read *counts* here and *times* from the counter-free
benches (`scripts/bench-ratio.sh`). Conflating the two wastes afternoons — see
`docs/benchmarking.md`, which exists because it did.

Counters need a `--features perf-stats` build; `make perf-brood` produces one. Without
it `:enabled?` is false and `:hint` says so, rather than reporting zeros as if they
were measurements."
  (:use math))

;; Fewest samples a rate must rest on before `verdict` will judge it. Coarse and
;; uncalibrated — the point is only that a rate from single digits is noise, not that 1000 is
;; the right line. Below it the rate is still *reported*; it just does not drive a label.
(def min-samples 1000)

;; ---- derived ratios ---------------------------------------------------------
;; Each returns nil rather than a number when its denominator is zero, so "no data"
;; is distinguishable from "measured zero" — the distinction the whole report rests on.

(defn- pct (x)
  "`x` as a percentage rounded to one decimal — for prose only. The map keeps full
  precision, because a consumer may want it and rounding is a display choice."
  (string/format "%.1f%%" (* 100.0 x)))

(defn- dp2 (x)
  "`x` rounded to 2 decimals, or nil unchanged — for prose only."
  (if (nil? x) nil (/ (->float (round (* 100.0 x))) 100.0)))

(defn- share (part whole)
  "`part / whole` as a float, or nil when `whole` is zero (no samples)."
  (if (or (nil? part) (nil? whole) (= whole 0))
    nil
    (/ (->float part) (->float whole))))

(defn- at (m k)
  "`(get m k)`, defaulting a missing counter to 0 — an absent key means the build
  does not track it, which for a ratio is the same as never having happened."
  (or (get m k) 0))

(defn ratios (vm gc)
  "Derived figures, split by what the counters can actually support.

**Meaningful ratios** — all three live inside the *interpreted* domain, so numerator and
denominator are counted by the same machinery: `:defer-rate` (share of activations that fell
back to the tree-walker — the deopt surface), `:call-ic-hit-rate`, `:env-hops-per-get` (chain
depth; deep chains are a lexical-addressing target).

**Counts, not ratios** — `:allocations`, `:gc-collections`, `:native-runs`, `:native-deopts`. Allocation is
deliberately *not* normalised by activations, because it cannot be: once an arm tiers to
native its iterations stop being counted while its allocations keep being counted (through
`brood_rt_cons`). Measured on a 200k-iteration list-building loop that went native:
`:alloc` 200017 against `:vm-apply` 197 — a ratio of 1025 that says nothing about the
program. `:native-runs` (tiered arms that ran to completion natively) is in the map so a reader can
see whether that is the situation, and `:native-deopts` beside it because a high deopt count
against a low run count is the separate `compiles but keeps falling off native` signal."
  (let (applies (at vm :vm-apply)
        defers (at vm :tw-defer)
        activations (+ applies defers)
        ic-hit (at vm :call-ic-hit)
        ic-miss (at vm :call-ic-miss))
    {:defer-rate (share defers activations)
     :call-ic-hit-rate (share ic-hit (+ ic-hit ic-miss))
     :env-hops-per-get (share (at vm :env-hops) (at vm :env-get))
     :allocations (at vm :alloc)
     :gc-collections (at gc :collections)
     :native-runs (at vm :jit-native)
     :native-deopts (at vm :jit-deopt)
     ;; How many samples each rate above rests on. `verdict` refuses to judge a rate drawn
     ;; from too few, because a rate is not a signal at small n: a region whose hot loop runs
     ;; natively leaves only a handful of IC probes behind, and 0 hits out of 3 is a 0% hit
     ;; rate that means nothing. Measured — that exact shape labelled a JIT'd list-building
     ;; loop `:dispatch-bound` before this guard existed.
     :samples {:activations activations
               :ic-probes (+ ic-hit ic-miss)
               :env-gets (at vm :env-get)}}))

;; ---- the verdict ------------------------------------------------------------

(defn verdict (rs)
  "`[verdict why]` for a `ratios` map: which bottleneck the counters support, and the numbers
behind the call.

Verdicts: `:dispatch-bound` (a bytecode VM / JIT removes this), `:env-bound` (a
lexical-addressing or capture-flattening target), `:balanced`, or `:no-data`.

**`:alloc-bound` is deliberately absent**, though `docs/benchmarking.md` §2 names it as one of
the three. These counters cannot establish it: the only available denominator stops counting
once the hot path goes native, while allocations do not (see `ratios`). Guessing anyway would
label every JIT'd tail loop alloc-bound. To answer that question, read `:allocations` and
`:gc-collections` against a *second run* of the same workload — or sweep `BROOD_GC_FLOOR`,
which is what actually distinguishes GC-frequency-bound from allocation-volume-bound.

**The thresholds below are first-cut heuristics, not calibrated cutoffs.** Every number they
read is in the report beside them, so a reader can disagree with the label without having to
re-derive the inputs."
  (let (n (or (get rs :samples) {})
        ;; `(or … {})` and `(or … 0)`: this is a public function documented as taking a `ratios`
        ;; map, and a caller who hands it something else should get a verdict rather than
        ;; `<=: expected number, got nil` raised three frames down.
        enough? (fn (k) (>= (or (get n k) 0) min-samples))
        defer (if (enough? :activations) (get rs :defer-rate) nil)
        ic (if (enough? :ic-probes) (get rs :call-ic-hit-rate) nil)
        hops (if (enough? :env-gets) (get rs :env-hops-per-get) nil))
    (cond
      (and (nil? defer) (nil? hops) (nil? ic))
        [:no-data
          ;; Report the counts; do NOT assert *why* nothing is judgeable. A rate is nil either
          ;; because it rests on too few samples or because it has none at all (a zero
          ;; denominator), and this branch cannot tell which. The earlier wording claimed
          ;; "fewer than N samples on every rate" — a guess, and a wrong one for
          ;; `{:samples {:activations 5000 …}}` with absent rates, where it reported a sample
          ;; shortage that had not happened. Guessing a cause is what this module exists not to do.
          (string/interp "no rate is judgeable at min-samples={min-samples} (activations {(or (get n :activations) 0)}, IC probes {(or (get n :ic-probes) 0)}, env gets {(or (get n :env-gets) 0)}) — either too few samples or none at all. A region whose hot path runs natively looks like this.")]
      (and defer (> defer 0.05))
        [:dispatch-bound (string/interp "{(pct defer)} of activations deferred to the tree-walker")]
      (and hops (> hops 3.0))
        [:env-bound (string/interp "{(dp2 hops)} env-chain frames walked per lookup")]
      (and ic (< ic 0.9))
        [:dispatch-bound (string/interp "call-site inline cache hit rate {(pct ic)}")]
      :else
        [:balanced
          (string/interp "no interpreted counter dominates ({(get rs :allocations)} allocations, {(get rs :gc-collections)} collections, {(get rs :native-runs)} native runs / {(get rs :native-deopts)} deopts — judge allocation separately)")])))

;; ---- the report ------------------------------------------------------------

(defn report ()
  "Triage the current process's counters in one call: `{:enabled? :verdict :why
:ratios :vm :gc :debug-build?}`.

`:enabled?` false means this binary has no counters compiled in (`:hint` says how to
get one) — the snapshots are still returned, but every counter reads zero and the
verdict is `:no-data`, never a claim.

`:debug-build?` true means the timings in `:gc` are from a debug binary and are not
representative; the *counts* still are."
  (let (vm (dev/vm-stats)
        gc (dev/gc-stats)
        on? (= (get vm :enabled) true)
        rs (ratios vm gc)
        v (verdict rs))
    {:enabled? on?
     :verdict (if on? (first v) :no-data)
     :why (if on?
            (first (rest v))
            "counters not compiled in — build with `make perf-brood`, then set BROOD_PERF_STATS=1")
     ;; The exact command, not a paraphrase — `make perf-brood` matches `release-brood`'s
     ;; flags, which a hand-rolled `cargo build` would not.
     :hint (if on?
             nil
             "make perf-brood   # then: BROOD_PERF_STATS=1 target/release-fast/brood prog.blsp")
     :ratios rs
     :vm vm
     :gc gc
     :debug-build? (= (get gc :debug-build) true)}))

(defn measure (thunk)
  "Zero the counters, run `thunk`, and report on **just that region**: `{:value :report}`.

Reach for this rather than a bare `(perf/report)` whenever you care about a specific piece
of work. The counters are process-global and cumulative from process start, so a plain
report on a short program also counts the runtime's own boot — and boot is
macro-expansion-heavy, which defers to the tree-walker. Measured: the same list-building
program read an **84% defer rate on a cold boot cache and 0.8% on a warm one**, purely from
whether expansion had to run. That is a property of the boot, not of the program, and
without a reset it is indistinguishable from one.

Two limits on `just that region`. The counters are still **process-global**, so a concurrent
process's work lands in them too — measure one workload at a time, or accept that the numbers
are the whole runtime's. And the **GC figures are not resettable**: there is no counterpart to
`(vm-stats-reset)` for `(gc-stats)`, so `:gc-collections` stays cumulative from process start
even inside a `measure`. Read it as a ceiling on the region, not a count of it."
  (dev/vm-stats-reset)
  (let (v (thunk))
    {:value v :report (report)}))

(defn- line (label value)
  (io/puts (string/interp "  {label}  {value}")))

(defn summary ()
  "Print `(perf/report)` for a human, then return it.

The shape a terminal reader wants: the verdict first, the numbers that produced it
next, then the raw snapshots' headline counters. Returns the map so it is still usable
from a script."
  (let (r (report))
    (io/puts (string/interp "perf: {(get r :verdict)} — {(get r :why)}"))
    (when (get r :debug-build?)
      (io/puts "  (debug build — treat the GC pause times as meaningless; counts are fine)"))
    (let (rs (get r :ratios))
      (line "defer rate       " (dp2 (get rs :defer-rate)))
      (line "call-IC hit rate " (dp2 (get rs :call-ic-hit-rate)))
      (line "env hops per get " (dp2 (get rs :env-hops-per-get)))
      (line "allocations      " (get rs :allocations))
      (line "gc collections   " (get rs :gc-collections))
      (line "native runs      " (get rs :native-runs))
      (line "native deopts    " (get rs :native-deopts)))
    r))
